### D1. Scraping legality for Swiss public procurement data (deepening)

- **No sui generis database right in Switzerland.** Confirmed by a Swiss law-firm deck (KPMG event, Dr. L. Ferrari Hofer): "No 'sui generis' ownership right for databases — in contrast to EU Directive 96/9/EC". Scraping a Swiss database therefore cannot infringe an EU-style maker's right; the residual levers are copyright (only for creative compilations), unfair competition (UWG Art. 5/6), trade secrets (StGB Art. 162), and StGB Art. 143 (unbefugter Datenbezug — mainly circumvention of technical barriers). Sources: https://www.swlegal.com/media/filer_public/7e/27/7e27996e-29dc-4d84-927d-27f1fcde3df5/220610_lorenza_ferrari_hofer_data_and_databases_status_quo_in_ch.pdf ; contrast https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Database_right
- **The underlying data is largely copyright-free.** URG (SR 231.1) Art. 5(1)(c): "Entscheidungen, Protokolle und Berichte von Behörden und öffentlichen Verwaltungen" are not copyright protected; Art. 5(2): official or legally mandated collections likewise. Art. 4 protects collections only where selection/arrangement is "eine geistige Schöpfung mit individuellem Charakter" — a chronological tender database is not. Verified against the official text (mirror: ccdigitallaw.ch URG PDF). So award notices as such carry no CH copyright.
- **Practical constraint stack for _3:** (1) simap API AGB (commercial use allowed, attribution notice, 08:00 embargo, no alteration — see verdict #4); (2) FADP for personal names (store company-level only — Round 1 risk stands); (3) rate/volume etiquette (undocumented limits — the API started throwing 400s during aggressive probing, so throttle to ≤1 req/s and cache); (4) for OpenTender-derived data, the CC BY-NC-SA license binds regardless of CH law (contract, not right) — Round 1's "prototype on OpenTender, production on simap/TED re-scrape" guidance stands, and is now stronger because the simap API is officially commercial-use-permitted.

### D2. Losing-bid proxies (deepening)

Public sources publish winner + value (+ n_bidders via simap/TED). Ways to get closer to losing-bid information:

1. **Participate-and-inspect (the main legal channel).** Swiss procurement law gives *participants* information rights: the award is formally notified to all participating tenderers (BöB Art. 51 «Zuschlagsverfügung» — confirmed via admin.ch procurement guide referencing "Art. 51 BöB"); involved bidders can typically inspect the award file, competitor offers redacted for business secrets, under the federal BöB/VöB regime and cantonal IVöB equivalents. So every tender Equans bids on yields, at zero marginal cost, winner price + n_bidders + (redacted) competitor structure — log these from day one via `_4_RecursiveImprovement` debriefs. (Exact inspection scope varies by canton — flag for counsel; not verifiable online with engines down.)
2. **Third-party freedom-of-information requests** (BGÖ for federal bodies; cantonal information laws for cantonal ones) can disclose award documentation incl. sometimes all bid values — slow, discretionary, secrets usually redacted. Treat as opportunistic, not pipeline.
3. **TED/eForms CANs** mandate "tenders received" (`received-submissions-type-val` in the Search API field list) but **not** runner-up identity or prices — confirmed by scanning the 1832 supported fields (no runner-up field exists). Some buyers voluntarily publish full bid rankings; not systematic in CH.
4. **simap award `numberOfSubmissions` + winner price per tender** (new, verified) — enough to fit per-client, per-sector bid-landscape distributions: P(win | our price) ≈ P(our price < min of N competitor draws) with N now observed rather than guessed.
5. **OpenTender 2006–2024** gives ~19 years of award values for priors on award/estimate ratios by canton/sector (NC license — prototyping only).
6. **Purchased competitor-price intelligence** (e.g. Tussell-class vendors) is EU/UK-centric with thin CH coverage; optional, low priority.
7. **Never** exchange bid-level data with named competitors directly — Kartellgesetz exposure; public-source + own-debrief data only.

### D3. Minimum viable dataset (deepening — what to log per tender from day one)

Aligned with what public sources actually return (verified field names in brackets) plus the internal fields only we have:

- Identity/joins: `publicationNumber` (e.g. 41332-02), `projectNumber`, simap `projectId`/`publicationId` UUIDs, TED publication-number for above-threshold, `ocid` for OpenTender joins, `cantonId`, `procOfficeName`/`buyer-name`, CPV/BKP/EBKP/NPK codes (simap exposes code-search endpoints `/api/codes/v1/{bkp,cpv,ebkp-h,ebkp-t,npk,oag}/search`).
- Market context: `publicationDate`, `newestPublicationFrom/Until` window used, `processType` (open/selective/invitation), `projectSubType`, `publicationTed` flag, **`numberOfSubmissions`** / `received-submissions-type-val`.
- Outcome: `decision.vendors[].vendorName` + `price{price,currency,vatType}`, `awardDecisionDate`, justification text (`awardDecisionJustification`) — the free-text "why" from the buyer side, ideal LLM-extraction input.
- Internal (the only losing-bid source): `our_bid_chf`, `cost_baseline_chf` (from `_2_Calculator` calc.json), `outcome`, `why` reason codes + narrative (from `_4` debriefs), capacity at bid time, client relationship/prior wins.
- Operational: `scraped_at`, `source`, raw JSON snapshot path (cache raw API responses verbatim — they are small and re-parseable; protects against churn).

Sibling agent 3's `bids.jsonl` schema already covers this well; the only Round-2 additions it needs: `n_bidders` is *publicly available* (add to every record), and `source` enum gains `simap_api` (not `simende`).

